[{:seon.task/name "one-commit-notice-carries-what-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Data: the commit notice (seon.writer/tx-notice), boot's one notice the same shape, carrying each commit's tx-data enough for load-diff! and the held db value, nothing re-read; one merge (the three-way paths deleted); one schema slot per process from the branch's entries; config as schema defaults plus datoms (task seon.config/config-is-schema-defaults-and-datoms). ONE MERGE, PLAN (data, 2026-11-16): PRIOR WORK [read]: the one merge is seon.merge/decide, carry and seon.writer/replay inside seon.writer/merge-main-into (write) and merge-into-main; the old three-way family is still called by seon.writer/branch-merge (the writer's :branch-diff, :merge-candidate), merge-into-main's checks (delta-three-way with base = theirs = main, i.e. delta-changes), and seon.reconcile's judge-tx (a three-way plus merge-tx carrying a judged branch's task content to main: a second merge). seon.merge/delta-changes is no merge (a branch's own change, which agents and status read) and stays. CHANGE, in order: (1) seon.writer/candidate, pure: main merged into a branch as a value (decide; clean: carry, replay, settle, cites), which merge-main-into writes and every reader takes; (2) :merge-candidate's value is the candidate (no merge-tx), :branch-diff's :ours is delta-changes and its conflicts decide's, :theirs dropped (no reader), merge-into-main's checks read delta-changes; (3) delete three-way, delta-three-way, merge-tx, carried-tx, tx-branch, owned?, shared?, overtaken-base, branch-merge; (4) last, a behaviour change with its own proof: judge-tx's carry goes, a branch's task content reaching main only by its :merge. COST: candidate O(d) as merge-main-into today; each caller's old three-way was O(main's churn since base) (15.3 s / 12.9 GB on a stale branch, a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step), so each moves to O(d)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merged-tree-is-analyzed-in-a-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: first measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing, delete unanalyzable instead; Optimization measures, numbers to the owner Sean before any merge-blocking check. From an independent audit of Context's merges (2026-11-08), approved by the owner. Done: a merge that leaves a name unbound (an octopus member calling what another removes, or one branch removing what main still calls) is refused from the recorded references, with no process (docs/merging.md#a-merge-leaves-no-name-unbound). Left: seon.writer/unanalyzable runs source/refresh-tx in root's own image, so before a merge is decided root evaluates the candidate's ns form (which can require), its defmacros, unaliases live namespaces and flips a live var's :private (seon.source/alias-held-not-declared guards one symptom). Learned 2026-11-08: a process loading the merged program from main's (seon.program/load-diff!, as an agent resumed on a branch does) does not see a removed name called as x/f, since Jolt compiles a name under an alias with no var as a lookup at each call; it does see an unqualified one and a changed macro's callers. A child that loads needs no new mechanism: an agent with no task spawned on a branch holding the candidate runs load-diff! at start and its hello carries :load-errors (root's set-agent would answer them with the spawn). Before building, measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import (analyzed in its own process when recorded) and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing seen, delete unanalyzable and alias-held-not-declared rather than move them. A merge-blocking check: its numbers go to the owner before it lands."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.writer-test/a-merged-tree-is-analyzed-in-a-fork}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-heap-is-collected-at-quiescence"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Optimization (2026-11-10, bisecting batch 12's red): root's :collect op (seon.writer/other-op) ends in seon.lib/non-static-live!, Chez's (collect (collect-maximum-generation)), which raises \"collect: cannot collect when multiple threads are active\" on every call in a running root (its Flow procs are threads), so root's heap is never collected in full at quiescence as docs/machine.md#what-a-process-owns-in-memory says, and every :collect is answered refused (the record of the store's collection is written first). Seen twice in one bin/check e2e calc. Learn first: Chez's collect-rendezvous (a collection any thread may request, run when every thread reaches a safe point) or collect-request-handler, as Jolt exposes them (Deep fixes owns seon.lib's Chez procs); the fork server calls the same proc where it is single-threaded at a fork. The cut: root asks for its full collection by the one that works with threads running; the answer is the bytes live after it, as now. Proof: seon.system-test/a-quiescent-root-collects-its-heap (a running root's :collect answers ok with its live bytes, never the multiple-threads refusal)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-quiescent-root-collects-its-heap}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change"}, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.writer-test/a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.writer/branch-diff {:n 1266, :order :linear, :ticks 40000000, :bytes 200000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (data, 2026-11-15 18:00, root's order: read the writer stall flow caught). FOUND [M]: on the standing root after the 17:26 pin restart, :writer stepped ONE :in message for 37.15 s (flow's ping, result/c7a3bfc3, recorded 17:32:59), which matches main's 17:32:11-17:32:49 gap with no commit; a sample of that step (/tmp/flow-writer-hang/sample.txt) is 98.5% CPU in konserve's per-key SHA-512 (key->store-key) plus LMDB mdb_get plus Chez GC: a walk reading many stored index nodes. Ruled out at root [M]: collection (last :seon.system/collected 15:37), merge commits (none on any branch in the window but :main/tests' boot merge at 17:26:53), task flights (no :seon.task/execution or outcome change), the union (no caller in src), a branch connect (1.07 ms, result/d1c830f7), merge-base (21-450 ms, result/b03b04c2). CAUSE (the class) [M]: the writer computes merges inside its step: :branch-diff and :merge-candidate (branch-merge, ``seon.merge/delta-three-way``), :merge-main and a resumed :spawn (merge-main-into), :octopus-merge. Each costs O(main's churn since the branch's base), not O(d_branch): :branch-diff's computation on :main/tests-meas6, a branch with ZERO facts of its own, took 15.3 s, 3B ticks, 12.9 GB (result/c6594a16; main moved ~33k txs since its base); the same on :main/tests as it stood at 17:32 (base at its boot merge) took 38 ms (result/d2836a1f). So it is not boot's O(store): it is any merge ask on a stale branch, and while it runs every write in the system waits. Which branch's ask the 37 s step was is [H]: the store records writes, not the message a step held; the ask was read-only (no write followed). PRIOR WORK: flow's approved verdict on landing 6 (seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change): the merge computes in an seon.fx worker over immutable dbs and the writer only applies, CAS on main's commit; seon.merge/decide (landing 6 slice 1, on jolt/data) judges the branch's facts only. FIX: (1) :branch-diff, :merge-candidate, :merge-main and :spawn's merge compute through one seon.fx effect over references {:branch :commit :t} (docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference); the writer applies the answer only if main and the branch are still at those commits, else the asker re-asks; (2) the computation is landing 6's decide (O(d_branch)) for every one of them, deleting branch-merge and delta-three-way's writer callers (landing 10). COST: n = d, the branch's facts since base; O(d) seeks on three dbs plus merge-base's commit walk; ~37k ticks a key [M landing 6]: a 1.3k-fact branch ~40M ticks, a branch with none ~ merge-base alone; lower bound one read of what the branch wrote. The writer's step for any merge then holds only the apply: O(d) checked ops. PROOF to write, red first: a :branch-diff of a branch whose base is far behind main answers while a main write asked after it is answered first (the writer is never held by a merge's read). ALSO (flow, 2026-11-15): a ping's site shows only the message that began a step (:flushed), never the batch drained inside it, so the 37 s step could not be captured after it ended. Fix at the cause: the writer's :ping-map-fn names the op kinds of the batch it is running ({:batch [:branch-for-tree :merge-main ...]}), so a long step names its own work and a capture is one ping."}

 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-writes-are-one-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (with context, 2026-11-16). MEASURED [M agent, result/ccdce674]: a commit of one datom allocates ~2.15 MB and ~4-6 ms CPU, warm, three times: a Datahike commit rewrites each index's path in the current and history trees (docs/storage.md: 34 nodes for 10 datoms), content-hashed, so it costs per commit, not per datom. Context's count [M result/dd8788aa at agent ne62bc71]: a fresh agent's first replies made 28 commits for 6 evals (51 over 6 there, ~8.5 an eval): the turn's open, record, basis and status writes each commit (seon.turn, seon.repl, seon.agent: 30 seon.agent/commit! call sites), against the agent's 5 MB own-memory bound. KEPT: one commit per eval (seon.agent-test/an-agent-commits-after-each-eval: a page shows each eval as it lands). PLAN: an eval's records and the writes its reply makes around it (turn open, basis, status) go in that eval's one commit: the call sites that commit their own small write mark the state dirty and the eval's commit takes them; the turn's end commits what is left. PRIOR WORK: seon.agent/commit!, its :dirty? flag (repl.clj already commits only when dirty at several sites), the per-eval rule. COST: estimate one commit an eval, ~2.2 MB; measured today ~8.5 an eval, ~18 MB. Proof, red first: an eval's reply makes one commit (count commit! calls over one eval at an agent), with an-agent-commits-after-each-eval still green. (This entry replaces seon.agent's of the same name, 2026-11-14, flow's then: its per-site reading is folded in here.)\n\nSTEP 1, on jolt/data (data, 2026-11-16) [M at a fresh agent on the standing system, i834d223cfec7175, commit! wrapped to count]: a session's one-eval reply made 8 agent commits plus main's merge on main's code (result/d1d9f6c0, result/a9b667ef: a delivery's own commit, two commits a request (basis, then hash and parts), the reply's record, the main heard at the read, the read's record), 4 plus the merge after (result/c0d7c4da: the turn's basis, the eval with the turn's request and the reply's record, the read's basis with the main heard, the read's request with its record). seon.agent/deliver! commits nothing of its own (a turn's basis follows it, an eval's commit follows `messages`); seon.turn/request! commits nothing, its callers do (a model turn before the call's wait, a read before its answer, an outside reply's eval); the reply's record is written before its evals. A model turn: 2 + k evals, was 4 + k. Kept: an eval's commit as it lands (a page shows it), a basis's commit (its request reads it by id). The one case that changes: a commit the store refuses (refused!) now drops the turn's request record with the eval's writes. Proof: seon.agent-test/an-evals-writes-are-one-commit (3 to 5 between two counter reads; 9 on main's code).\n\nLEFT, toward one commit a reply: the read's basis and request are two commits because a basis names a commit (its request is drawn from it by id); the turn's basis at the next reply's start is a third. One a reply needs a basis that the commit after it can hold (a basis by :t on the branch, which seon.render.ui already falls back to for a swept commit), context's design (seon.ctx.wire), filed with them before it is built. The second producer on agent branches is main's merge each reply (main moves ~every second): [H] main's own commits include agent status flips (:seon.agent/status :busy/:idle written on main at each eval), measured next."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-evals-writes-are-one-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-writers-flush-is-an-effect-with-a-deadline"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow, its proc side (seon.step/the-writers-store-calls-are-an-effect; owner, 2026-11-16: flow code is flow's alone); data keeps the writer's transaction logic. FOUND: the 30 s step at pid 52331's stop (runs/root.log.prev: ROOT STOP PHASE :fact {:ticks 3721 :ms 30568.9}) was the old stop's (stop! before 571907e0f8) wait on the stop fact's write, a transact-main! with {:flush? true}, on the writer: 3.7k ticks of the stopping thread, no fiber running at the stop's start (the writer steps on its own thread, which a fiber dump does not show), and the render proc holding a merge candidate's answer ({:r {:ok :db :base :base-t}}) unanswered, so a merge was in flight. The writer waited either in a merge computed in its step (task seon.writer/a-merge-is-computed-off-the-writers-step) or in its inline pdb/sync!: the record cannot tell which. A sync of the clean standing env measured 0.03 ms (14.6 GB store), so a 30 s sync needs a very large dirty set; the merge in the step is the likelier cause. Flow records the writer's step and out-port at an overrun since 433d42035f, so the next one is named; the restart of 2026-11-16 17:07 drained in 254 ms.\nPRIOR WORK: seon.writer/flusher-proc (the group flush, the one owner of the env sync), the writer's two inline syncs (its in-port closing, and :flush? requests: flush-now?), seon.step/put-after! (the one timer: an effect's declared deadline).\nDESIGN: the writer never syncs inline. A :flush? request's answer is held (as :clean-waiters are) until the flusher's :flushed covers its commit; the writer arms put-after! on its own in-port with {:op :flush-deadline :commits n} after :seon.config.store/sync-ms (a new spec'd key, default 5000), and a deadline that finds the commit still dirty answers the held requests with a typed :seon.writer/flush-overran and puts it on :events. The flusher answers on a writer in-port of its own ([:flusher :out] [:writer :flushed], resources/seon/system.edn, a build input), so at the stop, after :in closes, the writer still takes the flush's answer or its deadline before it says :drained. Deletes: the writer's two inline pdb/sync! calls (the flusher's is the one).\nAGREED WITH FLOW (2026-11-16): the blocking calls, the Datahike commit and the env sync, are one effect kind, :store, each op under its declared deadline. Request {:op :commit :conn-ref branch :ops :meta :rid} or {:op :sync :commits n :rid}; answer {:rid :ok true :t :commit :reports} or {:rid :ok true :synced n}, or a typed :seon.store/deadline or :seon.store/refused. (1) The writer's step returns: it outputs the request and keeps the batch pending in its state, and the answer arrives on its :answers in-port, where the next step goes on; no step waits. (2) One commit in flight: while one is pending, the writer's ::flow/input-filter reads only :answers, so commits stay ordered and its in-port gives back-pressure (the api's credit pattern). (3) The live connection stays in the effect worker (the kind's :open), never in a message: :conn-ref names the branch. The writer and flusher procs become :io fibers; the worker stays :mixed until deps' off-carrier FFI lands.\nESTIMATE: no new work on the path: one timer armed and cancelled a flush (0.2 us), the sync itself unchanged.\nPROOF, red first: a writer whose flusher never answers answers a :flush? request with :seon.writer/flush-overran, and its stop still drains."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-flush-past-its-deadline-is-a-typed-error}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.writer/writer-proc {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 1000, :bytes 10000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-merge-analyzes-its-changed-forms"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data. FOUND by flow (2026-11-16), reading the gate stage it deletes: seon.writer/unanalyzable refuses an unresolved name only for a namespace the given db does not hold. At the REPL, on a new namespace app.zz it refuses \"(no-such-fn-anywhere x)\" and a syntax error, and passes a missing alias (str/join with no require) [result/c520b1e8]. Inside a merge it refuses nothing: a branch redefining app.m0/f2 to call no-such-fn-anywhere (app.m0 with its :ns/file) merges through writer-proc's :merge both with (unanalyzable head text), what :merge-candidate does, and with (unanalyzable main text) [result/cb93fa3d, result/c7fba3e9]. Cause, flow's hypothesis to confirm: seon.source/refresh-tx analyzes only the forms that differ from the db it is given, so against the candidate (which already holds them) nothing is analyzed. So the gate's analysis never protected a merge.\nPRIOR WORK: seon.writer/unanalyzable, seon.source/refresh-tx and accounts/analyze-form (the import's own analysis), seon.writer/dangling-refused (a reference to no var, checked in merge-into-main).\nPLAN: every merge analyzes the forms its change carries, once, where the merge is computed (merge-into-main, beside dangling-refused): the changed forms' text read from the candidate's change (the one read of changed-datoms), each analyzed against the candidate's program (an unresolved name, a missing alias, a syntax error: a refusal naming the form), never a refresh of files against a db that already holds them. unanalyzable then has no caller and goes.\nESTIMATE: O(changed forms), one analysis each (the import's cost a form).\nPROOF, red first: a branch whose changed form calls a name that resolves nowhere is refused by the merge, naming the form; one calling an alias its namespace never required is refused too."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-merge-refuses-a-changed-form-that-does-not-analyze}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.writer/merge-into-main {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 3000000, :bytes 20000000}}} {:seon.task/name "the-merge-in-seon-done-costs-its-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (root's order 2026-11-16: the merge inside seon_done and its write-back, bound ~0.1 s, docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound). MEASURED [M 2026-11-16 ~21:10, a fresh agent pb5e04e61ce886fd on the standing store, its branch holding one rewritten deftest of test/seon/agent_test.clj imported, plus its session's own records: 52 source transactions, 389 ops; the change against main 144 datoms retracted and 421 added over 159 keys], each read-only step of seon.writer/merge-into-main (result/a1b06f54): the base 0.19M ticks; the candidate 21.5M / 78 MB / 86 ms; changed-datoms 0.76M / 2 MB; delta-changes 1.8M / 5.2 MB; merge-refused 2.3M / 3.4 MB; dangling-refused 0.75M / 2.1 MB; written 0.07M: ~27M ticks, ~92 MB, ~105 ms. The candidate by stage (result/d061b022): mapped 3.0M / 11.8 MB, decide 3.1M / 5.6 MB, carry 0.4M / 1.7 MB, replay 18.1M / 69.8 MB (52 transactions each through seon.db/with's check). WARM, three runs each (result/b9cd1d76; the first figures above are cold, and a cold read of the check, result/d880cf3e, said 8x, which warm runs withdraw): the candidate 18.9M / 39.4 MB / 66 ms; the replay 11.7M / 22.5 MB / 43 ms; the same transactions through Datahike's own with alone 8.4M / 14.9 MB / 28 ms, so the write check adds ~40%, its parts small (on the 149-op transaction: the datom check 36k, the entity check 55k, write-once 11k: result/c89d535c). Datahike's with, warm and unchained, costs ~19k ticks a transaction plus ~12.5k an op (92 transactions, 562 ops: 8.8M, result/b75a5328, result/abbc1ffb), against ~5k an op inserted. Most of a session branch's transactions are its own eval and turn records, which the merge carries onto main. THE WRITE-BACK: the merged candidate's text of the file (seon.doc/files) equals the worktree's byte for byte, 344,931 bytes, 9.6M ticks / 35 MB / 39 ms (result/aae67e48); seon.fx/git-commit of it with :expect on a scratch branch of a --shared clone of this repository, 6.4M ticks / 21.5 MB / 83 ms wall, 33 ms CPU (git's processes), its blob byte-equal and the one file changed (result/a7f9f2d1). So a one-form merge is ~225 ms and ~43M ticks before d/merge-db's commit, against ~0.1 s: 2.3x. ESTIMATE, the lower bound: the candidate is the branch's d datoms replayed once (~5k ticks a datom inserted, d=565: ~3M) plus the check of the entities it touches (~30 entities, ~20k ticks each: ~0.6M); the write-back's text one walk of the file's forms (~345 KB: ~2M); git's plumbing ~4 processes (~30 ms, the floor while git is a process). NEXT, by size (no part is past 10x its floor; the whole is ~2.2x the bound): (1) DONE 2026-11-16: git's commit in two processes (seon.fx/fast-commit, ~69 -> ~34 ms) and the tree id read from the commit (seon.fx/commit-tree-id, 293-465 -> 17 ms), docs/cost.md's write-back row; left there, the readability re-check of the drawn text, 5.9M ticks / 16 ms; (2) Datahike's with per op, ~12.5k ticks against ~5k (deps' Datahike); (3) the 52 source transactions replayed one by one: a session's eval and turn records are most of them, so fewer transactions an eval (seon.writer/an-evals-writes-are-one-commit) cuts the replay too; (4) mapped and decide read the branch's change twice (task seon.merge/the-one-merge-reads-each-fact-once)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
